From 1008cf71cdfb948270f2fc93ed2d1cf78f7d3b0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 16:09:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/59] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, nonCoherentAtomSize is a CPU
 cache property
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

nc_atom_size_B was the services' compression page size, on the reasoning
that it is the smallest unit the GPU's memory subsystem addresses
coherently. That is the wrong quantity. nonCoherentAtomSize bounds what
vkFlushMappedMemoryRanges and vkInvalidateMappedMemoryRanges touch, and
those run `dc cvac` / `dc civac` on the CPU, so it has to be the cache
write-back granule.

cache_ops.h:65 says so outright -- "Vulkan drivers should return this as
nonCoherentAtomSize" -- and the nouveau backend does exactly that
(nvkmd_nouveau_pdev.c:113).

The old value was not merely imprecise. GM20B reports a 0x20000
compression page, and nvk_device_memory.c:461 rounds a flush range UP to
this number before handing it to the cache ops. A 4 KiB allocation
flushed with VK_WHOLE_SIZE therefore had `dc civac` run across 128 KiB,
124 KiB past the end of the object -- a data abort if the pages beyond
it are not mapped, and silent maintenance of unrelated memory if they
are. t_vulkan flushes and invalidates its 4 KiB buffer with
VK_WHOLE_SIZE, so this is on the mandatory sequence's own path.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 item 2 (GM20B info -> nvkmd_pdev_info)
Why: nvkmd_pdev_info carries a CPU cache-maintenance granularity, and
  the Horizon backend filled it from a GPU characteristic of a
  completely different magnitude.
Evidence: t_init on console reports "compression_page=0x20000" (2026-07-27
  logs); nvk_device_memory.c:461 does ALIGN_POT(size, nc_atom_size_B)
  before nvkmd_mem_sync_client_map_to_gpu; cache_ops.h:65 states the
  intended source.
Upstream: no — this is the Horizon backend's own pdev.
---
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c | 29 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c
index 92ca298..1f88fce 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_pdev.c
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 #include "vulkan/vulkan_core.h"
 #include "vk_log.h"
 
+#include "util/cache_ops.h"
 #include "util/log.h"
 #include "util/macros.h"
 #include "util/u_memory.h"
@@ -166,13 +167,29 @@ fill_dev_info(struct nv_device_info *info,
    info->vram_size_B = 0;
    info->bar_size_B = 0;
 
-   /* The atom size for a non-coherent map. Horizon's NvMap allocations
-    * are page-granular and the CPU cache line on Cortex-A57 is 64 B;
-    * the compression page size the services report is the smallest
-    * unit the GPU's memory subsystem addresses coherently, so it is
-    * used rather than a constant.
+   /* The atom size for a non-coherent map: a CPU cache-maintenance
+    * property, not a GPU one.
+    *
+    * This used to be the services' compression page size, on the
+    * reasoning that it is the smallest unit the GPU's memory subsystem
+    * addresses coherently. That is the wrong quantity. nonCoherentAtomSize
+    * bounds what vkFlushMappedMemoryRanges and vkInvalidateMappedMemoryRanges
+    * touch, and those run `dc cvac` / `dc civac` on the CPU — so it has to
+    * be the cache write-back granule, which is what util_cache_granularity()
+    * reads out of CTR_EL0.CWG and what cache_ops.h says a Vulkan driver
+    * should report ("Vulkan drivers should return this as
+    * nonCoherentAtomSize", cache_ops.h:65). The nouveau backend uses
+    * exactly that (nvkmd_nouveau_pdev.c:113).
+    *
+    * The old value was not merely imprecise. GM20B reports a 0x20000
+    * compression page (measured: t_init on console, "compression_page=
+    * 0x20000"), and nvk_device_memory.c:461 rounds a flush range UP to
+    * this number before handing it to the cache ops. A 4 KiB allocation
+    * flushed with VK_WHOLE_SIZE therefore had `dc civac` run across
+    * 128 KiB — 124 KiB past the end of the object, which is a data abort
+    * if the pages beyond it are not mapped.
     */
-   info->nc_atom_size_B = gpu->compression_page_size;
+   info->nc_atom_size_B = util_cache_granularity();
 
    /* Tegra has no separate copy-engine queue exposed through `nv`; work
     * goes through the one GPFIFO channel. Advertising a transfer queue
-- 
